.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command emulates a CA application.
See the
.Sy WARNINGS
especially when considering to use it productively.
.Pp
It generates certificates bearing X.509 version 3. Unless specified
otherwise, key identifier extensions are included as described in
.Xr x509v3_config 5 .
.Pp
It can be used to sign certificate requests (CSRs) in a variety of forms
and generate certificate revocation lists (CRLs).
It also maintains a text database of issued certificates and their
status.
When signing certificates, a single request can be specified with the
.Sy -in
option, or multiple requests can be processed by specifying a set of
.Sy certreq
files after all options.
.Pp
Note that there are also very lean ways of generating certificates: the
.Sy req
and
.Sy x509
commands can be used for directly creating certificates.
See
.Xr openssl-req 1
and
.Xr openssl-x509 1
for details.
.Pp
The descriptions of the
.Sy ca
command options are divided into each purpose.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -verbose
This prints extra details about the operations being performed.
.It Sy -quiet
This prints fewer details about the operations being performed, which
may be handy during batch scripts or pipelines.
.It Sy -config Em filename
Specifies the configuration file to use.
Optional; for a description of the default value, see
.Xr openssl 1 .
.It Sy -name Em section Ns , Sy -section Em section
Specifies the configuration file section to use (overrides
.Sy default_ca
in the
.Sy ca
section).
.It Sy -in Em filename
An input filename containing a single certificate request (CSR) to be
signed by the CA.
.It Sy -inform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The format to use when loading certificate request (CSR) input files; by
default PEM is tried first.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -ss_cert Em filename
A single self-signed certificate to be signed by the CA.
.It Sy -spkac Em filename
A file containing a single Netscape signed public key and challenge and
additional field values to be signed by the CA. See the
.Sy SPKAC FORMAT
section for information on the required input and output format.
.It Sy -infiles
If present this should be the last option, all subsequent arguments are
taken as the names of files containing certificate requests.
.It Sy -out Em filename
The output file to output certificates to.
The default is standard output.
The certificate details will also be printed out to this file in PEM
format (except that
.Sy -spkac
outputs DER format).
.It Sy -outdir Em directory
The directory to output certificates to.
The certificate will be written to a filename consisting of the serial
number in hex with
.Pa .pem
appended.
.It Sy -cert Em filename
The CA certificate, which must match with
.Sy -keyfile .
.It Sy -certform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12
The format of the data in certificate input files; unspecified by
default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -keyfile Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The CA private key to sign certificate requests with.
This must match with
.Sy -cert .
.It Sy -keyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The format of the private key input file; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -sigopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm during sign operations.
Names and values of these options are algorithm-specific and documented
in
.Xr provider-signature 7 .
.It Sy -vfyopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm during verify operations.
Names and values of these options are algorithm-specific.
.Pp
This often needs to be given while signing too, because the
self-signature of a certificate signing request (CSR) is verified
against the included public key, and that verification may need its own
set of options.
.It Sy -key Em password
The password used to encrypt the private key.
Since on some systems the command line arguments are visible (e.g., when
using
.Xr ps 1
on Unix), this option should be used with caution.
Better use
.Sy -passin .
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The key password source for key files and certificate PKCS#12 files.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -selfsign
Indicates the issued certificates are to be signed with the key the
certificate requests were signed with (given with
.Sy -keyfile Ns ).
Certificate requests signed with a different key are ignored.
If
.Sy -spkac ,
.Sy -ss_cert
or
.Sy -gencrl
are given,
.Sy -selfsign
is ignored.
.Pp
A consequence of using
.Sy -selfsign
is that the self-signed certificate appears among the entries in the
certificate database (see the configuration option
.Sy database Ns ),
and uses the same serial number counter as all other certificates sign
with the self-signed certificate.
.It Sy -notext
Don't output the text form of a certificate to the output file.
.It Sy -dateopt
Specify the date output format.
Values are: rfc_822 and iso_8601. Defaults to rfc_822.
.It Sy -startdate Em date Ns , Sy -not_before Em date
This allows the start date to be explicitly set.
The format of the date is YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime
structure), or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 GeneralizedTime
structure).
In both formats, seconds SS and timezone Z must be present.
Alternatively, you can also use "today".
.It Sy -enddate Em date Ns , Sy -not_after Em date
This allows the expiry date to be explicitly set.
The format of the date is YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime
structure), or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (the same as an ASN1 GeneralizedTime
structure).
In both formats, seconds SS and timezone Z must be present.
Alternatively, you can also use "today".
.Pp
This overrides the
.Sy -days
option.
.It Sy -days Em arg
The number of days from today to certify the certificate for.
.Pp
Regardless of the option
.Sy -not_before ,
the days are always counted from today.
When used together with the option
.Sy -not_after Ns / Ns Sy -startdate ,
the explicit expiry date takes precedence.
.It Sy -md Em alg
The message digest to use.
Any digest supported by the
.Xr openssl-dgst 1
command can be used.
For signing algorithms that do not support a digest (i.e.
Ed25519 and Ed448) any message digest that is set is ignored.
This option also applies to CRLs.
.It Sy -policy Em arg
This option defines the CA "policy" to use.
This is a section in the configuration file which decides which fields
should be mandatory or match the CA certificate.
Check out the
.Sy POLICY FORMAT
section for more information.
.It Sy -msie_hack
This is a deprecated option to make this command work with very old
versions of the IE certificate enrollment control "certenr3". It used
UniversalStrings for almost everything.
Since the old control has various security bugs its use is strongly
discouraged.
.It Sy -preserveDN
Normally the DN order of a certificate is the same as the order of the
fields in the relevant policy section.
When this option is set the order is the same as the request.
This is largely for compatibility with the older IE enrollment control
which would only accept certificates if their DNs match the order of the
request.
This is not needed for Xenroll.
.It Sy -noemailDN
The DN of a certificate can contain the EMAIL field if present in the
request DN, however, it is good policy just having the e-mail set into
the altName extension of the certificate.
When this option is set the EMAIL field is removed from the certificate'
subject and set only in the, eventually present, extensions.
The
.Sy email_in_dn
keyword can be used in the configuration file to enable this behaviour.
.It Sy -batch
This sets the batch mode.
In this mode no questions will be asked and all certificates will be
certified automatically.
.It Sy -extensions Em section
The section of the configuration file containing certificate extensions
to be added when a certificate is issued (defaults to
.Sy x509_extensions
unless the
.Sy -extfile
option is used).
.Pp
See the
.Xr x509v3_config 5
manual page for details of the extension section format.
.It Sy -extfile Em file
An additional configuration file to read certificate extensions from
(using the default section unless the
.Sy -extensions
option is also used).
.It Sy -subj Em arg
Supersedes subject name given in the request.
.Pp
The arg must be formatted as
.Qo Li /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... Qc .
Special characters may be escaped by
.Qo Li \e Qc
(backslash), whitespace is retained.
Empty values are permitted, but the corresponding type will not be
included in the resulting certificate.
Giving a single
.Qo Li / Qc
will lead to an empty sequence of RDNs (a NULL-DN). Multi-valued RDNs
can be formed by placing a
.Qo Li + Qc
character instead of a
.Qo Li / Qc
between the AttributeValueAssertions (AVAs) that specify the members of
the set.
Example:
.Pp
.Qo Li /DC=org/DC=OpenSSL/DC=users/UID=123456+CN=John Doe Qc
.It Sy -utf8
This option causes field values to be interpreted as UTF8 strings, by
default they are interpreted as ASCII. This means that the field values,
whether prompted from a terminal or obtained from a configuration file,
must be valid UTF8 strings.
.It Sy -create_serial
If reading serial from the text file as specified in the configuration
fails, specifying this option creates a new random serial to be used as
next serial number.
To get random serial numbers, use the
.Sy -rand_serial
flag instead; this should only be used for simple error-recovery.
.It Sy -rand_serial
Generate a large random number to use as the serial number.
This overrides any option or configuration to use a serial number file.
.It Sy -multivalue-rdn
This option has been deprecated and has no effect.
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Sh CRL OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -gencrl
This option generates a CRL based on information in the index file.
.It Sy -crl_lastupdate Em time
Allows the value of the CRL's lastUpdate field to be explicitly set; if
this option is not present, the current time is used.
Accepts times in YYMMDDHHMMSSZ format (the same as an ASN1 UTCTime
structure) or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ format (the same as an ASN1
GeneralizedTime structure).
.It Sy -crl_nextupdate Em time
Allows the value of the CRL's nextUpdate field to be explicitly set; if
this option is present, any values given for
.Sy -crldays ,
.Sy -crlhours
and
.Sy -crlsec
are ignored.
Accepts times in the same formats as
.Sy -crl_lastupdate .
.It Sy -crldays Em num
The number of days before the next CRL is due.
That is the days from now to place in the CRL nextUpdate field.
.It Sy -crlhours Em num
The number of hours before the next CRL is due.
.It Sy -crlsec Em num
The number of seconds before the next CRL is due.
.It Sy -revoke Em filename
A filename containing a certificate to revoke.
.It Sy -valid Em filename
A filename containing a certificate to add a Valid certificate entry.
.It Sy -status Em serial
Displays the revocation status of the certificate with the specified
serial number and exits.
.It Sy -updatedb
Updates the database index to purge expired certificates.
.It Sy -crl_reason Em reason
Revocation reason, where
.Em reason
is one of:
.Sy unspecified ,
.Sy keyCompromise ,
.Sy CACompromise ,
.Sy affiliationChanged ,
.Sy superseded ,
.Sy cessationOfOperation ,
.Sy certificateHold
or
.Sy removeFromCRL .
The matching of
.Em reason
is case insensitive.
Setting any revocation reason will make the CRL v2.
.Pp
In practice
.Sy removeFromCRL
is not particularly useful because it is only used in delta CRLs which
are not currently implemented.
.It Sy -crl_hold Em instruction
This sets the CRL revocation reason code to
.Sy certificateHold
and the hold instruction to
.Em instruction
which must be an OID. Although any OID can be used only
.Sy holdInstructionNone
(the use of which is discouraged by RFC2459)
.Sy holdInstructionCallIssuer
or
.Sy holdInstructionReject
will normally be used.
.It Sy -crl_compromise Em time
This sets the revocation reason to
.Sy keyCompromise
and the compromise time to
.Em time .
.Em time
should be in GeneralizedTime format that is
.Em YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ .
.It Sy -crl_CA_compromise Em time
This is the same as
.Sy crl_compromise
except the revocation reason is set to
.Sy CACompromise .
.It Sy -crlexts Em section
The section of the configuration file containing CRL extensions to
include.
If no CRL extension section is present then a V1 CRL is created, if the
CRL extension section is present (even if it is empty) then a V2 CRL is
created.
The CRL extensions specified are CRL extensions and
.Sy not
CRL entry extensions.
It should be noted that some software (for example Netscape) can't
handle V2 CRLs.
See
.Xr x509v3_config 5
manual page for details of the extension section format.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION FILE OPTIONS
The section of the configuration file containing options for this
command is found as follows: If the
.Sy -name
command line option is used, then it names the section to be used.
Otherwise the section to be used must be named in the
.Sy default_ca
option of the
.Sy ca
section of the configuration file (or in the default section of the
configuration file).
Besides
.Sy default_ca ,
the following options are read directly from the
.Sy ca
section:
.br
RANDFILE
.br
preserve
.br
msie_hack With the exception of
.Sy RANDFILE ,
this is probably a bug and may change in future releases.
.Pp
Many of the configuration file options are identical to command line
options.
Where the option is present in the configuration file and the command
line the command line value is used.
Where an option is described as mandatory then it must be present in the
configuration file or the command line equivalent (if any) used.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy oid_file
This specifies a file containing additional
.Sy OBJECT IDENTIFIERS .
Each line of the file should consist of the numerical form of the object
identifier followed by whitespace then the short name followed by
whitespace and finally the long name.
.It Sy oid_section
This specifies a section in the configuration file containing extra
object identifiers.
Each line should consist of the short name of the object identifier
followed by
.Sy =
and the numerical form.
The short and long names are the same when this option is used.
.It Sy new_certs_dir
The same as the
.Sy -outdir
command line option.
It specifies the directory where new certificates will be placed.
Mandatory.
.It Sy certificate
The same as
.Sy -cert .
It gives the file containing the CA certificate.
Mandatory.
.It Sy private_key
Same as the
.Sy -keyfile
option.
The file containing the CA private key.
Mandatory.
.It Sy RANDFILE
At startup the specified file is loaded into the random number
generator, and at exit 256 bytes will be written to it.
(Note: Using a RANDFILE is not necessary anymore, see the
.Sx HISTORY
section.
.It Sy default_days
The same as the
.Sy -days
option.
The number of days from today to certify a certificate for.
.It Sy default_startdate
The same as the
.Sy -startdate
option.
The start date to certify a certificate for.
If not set the current time is used.
.It Sy default_enddate
The same as the
.Sy -enddate
option.
Either this option or
.Sy default_days
(or the command line equivalents) must be present.
.It Sy default_crl_hours default_crl_days
The same as the
.Sy -crlhours
and the
.Sy -crldays
options.
These will only be used if neither command line option is present.
At least one of these must be present to generate a CRL.
.It Sy default_md
The same as the
.Sy -md
option.
Mandatory except where the signing algorithm does not require a digest
(i.e.
Ed25519 and Ed448).
.It Sy database
The text database file to use.
Mandatory.
This file must be present though initially it will be empty.
.It Sy unique_subject
If the value
.Sy yes
is given, the valid certificate entries in the database must have unique
subjects.
if the value
.Sy no
is given, several valid certificate entries may have the exact same
subject.
The default value is
.Sy yes ,
to be compatible with older (pre 0.9.8) versions of OpenSSL. However, to
make CA certificate roll-over easier, it's recommended to use the value
.Sy no ,
especially if combined with the
.Sy -selfsign
command line option.
.Pp
Note that it is valid in some circumstances for certificates to be
created without any subject.
In the case where there are multiple certificates without subjects this
does not count as a duplicate.
.It Sy serial
A text file containing the next serial number to use in hex.
Mandatory.
This file must be present and contain a valid serial number.
.It Sy crlnumber
A text file containing the next CRL number to use in hex.
The crl number will be inserted in the CRLs only if this file exists.
If this file is present, it must contain a valid CRL number.
.It Sy x509_extensions
A fallback to the
.Sy -extensions
option.
.It Sy crl_extensions
A fallback to the
.Sy -crlexts
option.
.It Sy preserve
The same as
.Sy -preserveDN
.It Sy email_in_dn
The same as
.Sy -noemailDN .
If you want the EMAIL field to be removed from the DN of the certificate
simply set this to 'no'.
If not present the default is to allow for the EMAIL filed in the
certificate's DN.
.It Sy msie_hack
The same as
.Sy -msie_hack
.It Sy policy
The same as
.Sy -policy .
Mandatory.
See the
.Sy POLICY FORMAT
section for more information.
.It Sy name_opt Ns , Sy cert_opt
These options allow the format used to display the certificate details
when asking the user to confirm signing.
All the options supported by the
.Sy x509
utilities
.Sy -nameopt
and
.Sy -certopt
switches can be used here, except the
.Sy no_signame
and
.Sy no_sigdump
are permanently set and cannot be disabled (this is because the
certificate signature cannot be displayed because the certificate has
not been signed at this point).
.Pp
For convenience the values
.Sy ca_default
are accepted by both to produce a reasonable output.
.Pp
If neither option is present the format used in earlier versions of
OpenSSL is used.
Use of the old format is
.Sy strongly
discouraged because it only displays fields mentioned in the
.Sy policy
section, mishandles multicharacter string types and does not display
extensions.
.It Sy copy_extensions
Determines how extensions in certificate requests should be handled.
If set to
.Sy none
or this option is not present then extensions are ignored and not copied
to the certificate.
If set to
.Sy copy
then any extensions present in the request that are not already present
are copied to the certificate.
If set to
.Sy copyall
then all extensions in the request are copied to the certificate: if the
extension is already present in the certificate it is deleted first.
See the
.Sy WARNINGS
section before using this option.
.Pp
The main use of this option is to allow a certificate request to supply
values for certain extensions such as subjectAltName.
.El
.Sh POLICY FORMAT
The policy section consists of a set of variables corresponding to
certificate DN fields.
If the value is "match" then the field value must match the same field
in the CA certificate.
If the value is "supplied" then it must be present.
If the value is "optional" then it may be present.
Any fields not mentioned in the policy section are silently deleted,
unless the
.Sy -preserveDN
option is set but this can be regarded more of a quirk than intended
behaviour.
.Sh SPKAC FORMAT
The input to the
.Sy -spkac
command line option is a Netscape signed public key and challenge.
This will usually come from the
.Sy KEYGEN
tag in an HTML form to create a new private key.
It is however possible to create SPKACs using
.Xr openssl-spkac 1 .
.Pp
The file should contain the variable SPKAC set to the value of the SPKAC
and also the required DN components as name value pairs.
If you need to include the same component twice then it can be preceded
by a number and a '.'.
.Pp
When processing SPKAC format, the output is DER if the
.Sy -out
flag is used, but PEM format if sending to stdout or the
.Sy -outdir
flag is used.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Note: these examples assume that the directory structure this command
assumes is already set up and the relevant files already exist.
This usually involves creating a CA certificate and private key with
.Xr openssl-req 1 ,
a serial number file and an empty index file and placing them in the
relevant directories.
.Pp
To use the sample configuration file below the directories
.Pa demoCA ,
.Pa demoCA/private
and
.Pa demoCA/newcerts
would be created.
The CA certificate would be copied to
.Pa demoCA/cacert.pem
and its private key to
.Pa demoCA/private/cakey.pem .
A file
.Pa demoCA/serial
would be created containing for example "01" and the empty index file
.Pa demoCA/index.txt .
.Pp
Sign a certificate request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ca -in req.pem -out newcert.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Sign an SM2 certificate request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ca -in sm2.csr -out sm2.crt -md sm3 \e
         -sigopt "distid:1234567812345678" \e
         -vfyopt "distid:1234567812345678"
.Ed
.Pp
Sign a certificate request, using CA extensions:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ca -in req.pem -extensions v3_ca -out newcert.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a CRL
.Bd -literal
 openssl ca -gencrl -out crl.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Sign several requests:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ca -infiles req1.pem req2.pem req3.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Certify a Netscape SPKAC:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ca -spkac spkac.txt
.Ed
.Pp
A sample SPKAC file (the SPKAC line has been truncated for clarity):
.Bd -literal
 SPKAC=MIG0MGAwXDANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAANLADBIAkEAn7PDhCeV/xIxUg8V70YRxK2A5
 CN=Steve Test
 emailAddress=steve@openssl.org
 0.OU=OpenSSL Group
 1.OU=Another Group
.Ed
.Pp
A sample configuration file with the relevant sections for this command:
.Bd -literal
 [ ca ]
 default_ca      = CA_default            # The default ca section

 [ CA_default ]

 dir            = ./demoCA              # top dir
 database       = $dir/index.txt        # index file.
 new_certs_dir  = $dir/newcerts         # new certs dir

 certificate    = $dir/cacert.pem       # The CA cert
 serial         = $dir/serial           # serial no file
 #rand_serial    = yes                  # for random serial#'s
 private_key    = $dir/private/cakey.pem# CA private key

 default_days   = 365                   # how long to certify for
 default_crl_days= 30                   # how long before next CRL
 default_md     = sha256                # md to use

 policy         = policy_any            # default policy
 email_in_dn    = no                    # Don't add the email into cert DN

 name_opt       = ca_default            # Subject name display option
 cert_opt       = ca_default            # Certificate display option
 copy_extensions = none                 # Don't copy extensions from request

 [ policy_any ]
 countryName            = supplied
 stateOrProvinceName    = optional
 organizationName       = optional
 organizationalUnitName = optional
 commonName             = supplied
 emailAddress           = optional
.Ed
.Sh FILES
Note: the location of all files can change either by compile time
options, configuration file entries, environment variables or command
line options.
The values below reflect the default values.
.Bd -literal
 /usr/local/ssl/lib/openssl.cnf - master configuration file
 ./demoCA                       - main CA directory
 ./demoCA/cacert.pem            - CA certificate
 ./demoCA/private/cakey.pem     - CA private key
 ./demoCA/serial                - CA serial number file
 ./demoCA/serial.old            - CA serial number backup file
 ./demoCA/index.txt             - CA text database file
 ./demoCA/index.txt.old         - CA text database backup file
 ./demoCA/certs                 - certificate output file
.Ed
.Sh RESTRICTIONS
The text database index file is a critical part of the process and if
corrupted it can be difficult to fix.
It is theoretically possible to rebuild the index file from all the
issued certificates and a current CRL: however there is no option to do
this.
.Pp
V2 CRL features like delta CRLs are not currently supported.
.Pp
Although several requests can be input and handled at once it is only
possible to include one SPKAC or self-signed certificate.
.Sh BUGS
This command is quirky and at times downright unfriendly.
.Pp
The use of an in-memory text database can cause problems when large
numbers of certificates are present because, as the name implies the
database has to be kept in memory.
.Pp
This command really needs rewriting or the required functionality
exposed at either a command or interface level so that a more
user-friendly replacement could handle things properly.
The script
.Sy CA.pl
helps a little but not very much.
.Pp
Any fields in a request that are not present in a policy are silently
deleted.
This does not happen if the
.Sy -preserveDN
option is used.
To enforce the absence of the EMAIL field within the DN, as suggested by
RFCs, regardless the contents of the request' subject the
.Sy -noemailDN
option can be used.
The behaviour should be more friendly and configurable.
.Pp
Canceling some commands by refusing to certify a certificate can create
an empty file.
.Sh WARNINGS
This command was originally meant as an example of how to do things in a
CA. Its code does not have production quality.
It was not supposed to be used as a full blown CA itself, nevertheless
some people are using it for this purpose at least internally.
When doing so, specific care should be taken to properly secure the
private key(s) used for signing certificates.
It is advisable to keep them in a secure HW storage such as a smart card
or HSM and access them via a suitable engine or crypto provider.
.Pp
This command is effectively a single user command: no locking is done on
the various files and attempts to run more than one
.Sy openssl ca
command on the same database can have unpredictable results.
.Pp
The
.Sy copy_extensions
option should be used with caution.
If care is not taken then it can be a security risk.
For example if a certificate request contains a basicConstraints
extension with CA:TRUE and the
.Sy copy_extensions
value is set to
.Sy copyall
and the user does not spot this when the certificate is displayed then
this will hand the requester a valid CA certificate.
This situation can be avoided by setting
.Sy copy_extensions
to
.Sy copy
and including basicConstraints with CA:FALSE in the configuration file.
Then if the request contains a basicConstraints extension it will be
ignored.
.Pp
It is advisable to also include values for other extensions such as
.Sy keyUsage
to prevent a request supplying its own values.
.Pp
Additional restrictions can be placed on the CA certificate itself.
For example if the CA certificate has:
.Bd -literal
 basicConstraints = CA:TRUE, pathlen:0
.Ed
.Pp
then even if a certificate is issued with CA:TRUE it will not be valid.
.Sh HISTORY
Since OpenSSL 1.1.1, the program follows RFC5280. Specifically,
certificate validity period (specified by any of
.Sy -startdate ,
.Sy -enddate
and
.Sy -days )
and CRL last/next update time (specified by any of
.Sy -crl_lastupdate ,
.Sy -crl_nextupdate ,
.Sy -crldays ,
.Sy -crlhours
and
.Sy -crlsec )
will be encoded as UTCTime if the dates are earlier than year 2049
(included), and as GeneralizedTime if the dates are in year 2050 or
later.
.Pp
OpenSSL 1.1.1 introduced a new random generator (CSPRNG) with an
improved seeding mechanism.
The new seeding mechanism makes it unnecessary to define a RANDFILE for
saving and restoring randomness.
This option is retained mainly for compatibility reasons.
.Pp
The
.Sy -section
option was added in OpenSSL 3.0.0.
.Pp
The
.Sy -multivalue-rdn
option has become obsolete in OpenSSL 3.0.0 and has no effect.
.Pp
The
.Sy -engine
option was deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
Since OpenSSL 3.2, generated certificates bear X.509 version 3, and key
identifier extensions are included by default.
